<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charente</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Charente River</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Charente is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, about 380 kilometres long, which rises in the Limousin and flows past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angouleme">Angoulême</a>, Cognac and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintes">Saintes</a> to the Atlantic near Rochefort. In antiquity its valley was the land of the Santones, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it formed the heart of the county of Angoulême and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>. It gives its name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a> of Charente and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente-maritime">Charente-Maritime</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charente.jpg" alt="Charente" width="320" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jack ma · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ang_prefect.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
